<h2>Does exercise raise or lower cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>Exercise can change cortisol, but “lower” is not always the goal.</strong> A temporary rise during a demanding workout can be part of the body's response to exertion. It does not show that exercise is harming you or that your cortisol remains high all day.</p>
<p><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18787373/">Hill and colleagues studied exercise intensity and circulating cortisol</a>. Their study helps illustrate why intensity and sampling conditions matter; it does not establish a universal cortisol-lowering workout prescription.</p>
<h2>Walking, cardio, yoga and strength training</h2>
<p>Choose movement you can repeat and recover from. Walking, cycling, swimming, strength work and gentler movement can fit different abilities and preferences. You do not need to interpret every workout through a hormone score.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://odphp.health.gov/our-work/nutrition-physical-activity/physical-activity-guidelines/current-guidelines">US Physical Activity Guidelines</a> recommend 150–300 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week, or the vigorous equivalent, plus muscle-strengthening activity on at least two days for adults. Start with smaller amounts if needed. These recommendations concern overall health, not a guaranteed cortisol reduction.</p>
<h3>Walking and easy cardio</h3>
<p>A short walk can be a practical starting point when you are busy or returning to activity. Pick a pace and duration that fit your current capacity. Track how you feel and whether the routine is sustainable, rather than promising yourself a particular percentage drop in cortisol.</p>
<h3>Yoga and gentler movement</h3>
<p>If you enjoy yoga, stretching or a gentle class, build it around comfort and consistency. “Restorative,” “power” and “hot” classes differ in demands. A label alone does not tell you what will happen to your hormone level.</p>
<h3>Resistance training</h3>
<p>Plan manageable sessions and allow recovery. If a program repeatedly leaves you unable to function normally, reconsider the load with a qualified trainer or clinician. More exercise is not automatically a better response to feeling stressed.</p>
<h2>Is HIIT bad for cortisol?</h2>
<p>High-intensity exercise is not automatically bad. The useful question is whether its demand fits your training history, other activity and recovery. A wearable score does not determine the right number of hard sessions for everyone.</p>
<h2>When to reconsider training load</h2>
<p>Persistent fatigue, declining performance, poor sleep or changes in mood deserve attention, but none identifies an endocrine problem on its own. The <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23247672/">ECSS/ACSM overtraining consensus</a> describes a complex diagnosis that requires consideration of other causes. There is no single generally accepted biomarker test that makes this diagnosis.</p>
<p>Do not use a fixed HRV percentage, a few days of resting heart-rate change or an app score as proof of overtraining. Get professional help for persistent unexplained symptoms.</p>
<h2>A week of observation</h2>
<ol><li>Choose a realistic activity schedule rather than adding every possible session.</li><li>Note the type of activity and how demanding it felt.</li><li>Record sleep and how you feel the following day.</li><li>Adjust gradually, taking symptoms and recovery seriously.</li></ol>
<p>Our <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">seven-day journal</a> provides space for these observations. It is an observation tool, not a treatment protocol.</p>
<h2>How wearable tracking can help</h2>
<p>Cortisol+ can place available HRV, sleep and activity data together for reflection. It cannot verify hormone reduction, diagnose overtraining or predict symptoms weeks before they happen. See <a href="/how-it-works/">how the score is calculated</a> and <a href="/features/hrv-stress-monitoring/">HRV stress monitoring</a>.</p>
